WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:04.910
SAM BERLIN: Hi.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.520
I'm Sam, and this is Maurice.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.660
We're both leads
on the Sheets team.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.430
I lead the creation
of the Sheets B4 API,

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.670
and Maurice led the Slides API.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.320
And we're here to
talk to you today

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.170
about using Google Apps
Script and the G Suite editors

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.470
to make your life easier,
automate internal processes,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.190
and really just make things
simpler and better for you.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:34.570
So we're going to walk
through some demos today.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.890
We're going to give an
appetizer demo and then

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:40.185
a longer run-through
of a process

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:43.340
that an HR person might
use to hire people.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.984
And I'll let Maurice talk about
his demo when it comes to him.

00:00:46.984 --> 00:00:48.650
And our goal today
is really to show you

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:53.180
how you can use App Script
and spend less money getting

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.210
the stuff you need to do done.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.470
Companies spend billions
of dollars a year

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.026
automating their workflows.

00:01:02.026 --> 00:01:03.650
And that's not even
mentioning the time

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.750
they spend figuring
out how to do it.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.370
They have to contact
people to build it and work

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:14.490
through everything in
order to get it all done.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.280
With Apps Script, we can
take a lot of the pain away.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.670
These are some stories
of some customers

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.970
that have used Apps Script
to make their life better.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.540
My most favorite one, by
far, is the very last one,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.100
where a company simplified
a horrible revision merge

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.800
nightmare of taking all
these different files,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.910
merging them together, and then
creating some kind of output.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.845
And they simplified
their lives by using

00:01:42.845 --> 00:01:47.300
G Suite to automate it,
generating a PDF at the end.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.900
And what previously took
about 40 hours a month now

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.600
takes under three
and saves them,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.330
they estimated, over
$100,000 a year.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.720
And a customer literally said--
this is a natural quote--

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.040
finally I can take a vacation.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.500
So our goal here
is for you to be

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.690
able to take a vacation
from automating

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.430
all of these workflows.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.380
So here's a simple example.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.560
Everyone likes parties, right?

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.960
So imagine that you're
throwing an office party,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.190
and you want to send out RSVPs.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.695
You want to analyze
who responds to it,

00:02:19.695 --> 00:02:21.320
what parts of the
company they're from.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.960
Are they from sales, are
they from engineering?

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.580
Book a venue based
on the results.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.579
Do you have enough people
that can fit into one?

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:29.960
Do you need to split it
up into multiple venues?

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.990
What kinds of places do
people like to go to?

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.540
Traditionally, this would
be a lot of manual labor.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.550
You'd have to pore
through all the results,

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.476
figure out how many
people there are,

00:02:39.476 --> 00:02:40.850
where they belong
in the company,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.775
maybe what office they're from,
where you can host things.

00:02:43.775 --> 00:02:46.400
Or instead of doing that, you'd
purchase some turnkey solution,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.410
which would cost a lot of money.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.957
But realistically,
office parties

00:02:50.957 --> 00:02:52.040
aren't all that important.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.760
So you'd probably just
have some poor person

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.590
doing the most
minimal thing possible

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.860
and throwing a
rather generic party.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.590
With App Scripts
and Docs editors,

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:04.620
things are pretty easy.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.310
You can create a Google
Form, collect user names

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.030
when sending out the form,
get all your results back,

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.120
and associate the
form with the sheet.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.130
In Forms itself, you can
analyze all the responses.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:20.010
You can see 66% of people
said yes, they're in.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.734
33% said, no, they're not.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.900
And in Sheets, it'll sync
right back up into Sheets,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.210
and you can look and
do analysis in Sheets

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.580
if you want to do it in
a slightly manual way,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:33.110
maybe using Sheets Explore
or asking questions of it.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.540
But you can also
create an Apps Script

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.690
associated with the sheet in
order to do things for you.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.050
So here's a 20-line
example of how

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:46.190
you could create a very
straightforward app script that

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.550
looks at all of your data
in the sheet and figures out

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.966
who's in sales,
who's in engineering.

00:03:51.966 --> 00:03:54.590
So I'll go through each line and
explain what's happening here.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.540
So at the top, we
use the Groups app.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:01.170
And we get the eng@mycompany.org
and the sales@mycompany.org

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.206
groups.

00:04:02.206 --> 00:04:03.830
And then we get the
active spreadsheet,

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.890
because the script is
associated with the spreadsheet.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.780
And we get the data
from that spreadsheet

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.000
up until the last row of data.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.310
And we start the engineering
and sales counts at 0

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.100
and then loop through
all of our data.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.310
And we say is this
user in engineering--

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:22.010
by asking is the engGroup-- does
this engGroup have this user's

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.180
email address.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.740
If not, are they
in the salesGroup?

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.540
Does it have that
user's email address?

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.150
And we just increment the sales
and the engineering variables

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.050
by one.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.877
And then at the very end, after
we've collected all of that,

00:04:32.877 --> 00:04:35.210
we go back to the spreadsheet,
and we get the sheet name

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.470
with results.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.720
And we get a range where
we want to put the data in.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.700
We say this is how
many people were eng,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.740
this is how many
people were in sales.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.200
And so this is a very
straightforward example

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.930
of how you can use Apps Script
just analyzing your data

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:51.050
sheets automatically,
associating it with groups,

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.850
and putting your data
back in the sheet.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.970
So all of this
code is JavaScript.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.220
It's all stored in the
cloud with revision history.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.670
So if you make a mistake, you
can go back in time and fix it.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.720
And it's hosted and
run in the cloud, too.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.450
So you don't have
to spin up any jobs,

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.940
have maintenance deal with
having all of your servers

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.880
running, seeing if anything
goes wrong with them.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.440
And it's all secure, and
it comes with G Suite.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.590
But automating parties
isn't what brought you here.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.420
Businesses have a lot
more critical tasks,

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.070
like hiring people.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.500
There are a lot of
steps to hiring someone.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.970
You have to send offer letters.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.490
You have to collect
employee information.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.430
You have to have them sign NDAs
and verify their employment

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.010
history, and so on and so forth.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.800
So I'm going to walk through a
demo of how you could automate

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.320
the process of hiring someone
if you can switch to the demo

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.350
machine.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.980
So here's a spreadsheet.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.970
And I filled it in with some
basic examples here of employee

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.760
name, salary, manager
name, the team, and whether

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.240
or not an offer letter was
sent, the offer letter URL,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.547
if they signed an NDA,
and so on and so forth.

00:05:57.547 --> 00:05:59.880
There's an extra column in
here for their email address,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:00.838
which I've kept hidden.

00:06:00.838 --> 00:06:04.410
Because I'm going to,
right now, actually

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.240
make this an interactive demo.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.090
And if you'd like to, you can
see that there is a URL here--

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:13.964
goo.gl, capital
PDW, capital EQT.

00:06:13.964 --> 00:06:16.380
And if you want, you can go
to this place and fill it out.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.004
Just remember, it's
all being recorded,

00:06:18.004 --> 00:06:20.250
and you can fill out a
fake name if you want to

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.960
onto the employee tracking bit.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.730
So the idea here is that-- let's
say there is an HR manager.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.100
And they have this
spreadsheet with their data

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.440
and a form for new hires.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.560
And they want to send this
out to hiring managers

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.110
and say is there anyone
that you want to hire.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.690
If so, fill out their
email address, their name,

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.730
their salary,
their manager name.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:46.000
You can look what the form
looks like from the manager's

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:46.500
perspective.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.291
And it's just here.

00:06:47.291 --> 00:06:50.220
You see a little form where
they can type the stuff in

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.190
and submit the results.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.930
Others-- let's go
to Google Forms.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.010
And the form is synced
up with a spreadsheet.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.996
So the results come
into the spreadsheet.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.580
At this point, you can click
on this Workflows, which

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:10.610
is this custom menu item that
you added with Apps Script,

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.250
and click Send
New Offer Letters.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.170
What that's going
to do is iterate

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:18.140
through the spreadsheet, similar
to how we did the party example

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.460
and create a
customized Google Doc

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.500
for the employee of
their offer letter,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.360
and then email it to
them with the link

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:30.010
to their customized Google Doc.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:33.899
Afterwards, it'll mail
that to the employee.

00:07:33.899 --> 00:07:36.440
And the employee will basically
see, in their email, the link

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.106
to their doc and be
able to click on it.

00:07:38.106 --> 00:07:40.610
And so I'll show you
what that looks like.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.310
So here's one user.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:43.660
It says you're hired.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:46.099
Dear John Doe,
congratulations, you're hired.

00:07:46.099 --> 00:07:48.640
Please view your offer letter,
which includes the next steps,

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.010
at this URL.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.650
You open the URL, and you
see this customized Google

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.010
Doc for John Doe.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.780
And it has their
salary, their name.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.140
The title is specific
for John Doe.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:03.700
And it says to
continue the process,

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:05.380
please fill out this NDA.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.620
So we click on this link.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.320
This NDA is hosted
by App Script.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.999
App Script is acting as a
web app in this scenario.

00:08:12.999 --> 00:08:14.040
And here's where it goes.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.270
It says here's an NDA.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.210
Click here to sign it.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.550
When you sign it, it
comes back and says thanks

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:20.630
for signing the NDA.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:23.070
And that was also
hosted by App Script.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.610
And you can see that
the Apps Script then

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:32.909
filled in that yes, the
NDA was signed by John Doe.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:36.270
And none of the others
yet had filled it out.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.750
And if, for example, I
see some new people filled

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.526
out the workflow or the form--

00:08:42.526 --> 00:08:44.400
so if you click Send
New Offer Letters again,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.880
it will figure out who hasn't
had an offer letter sent

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.190
and send new offer letters
to each of these people,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:55.080
and update with the yes
and the customized Google

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.360
Doc for each of them.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.010
So I'm going to walk through the
code that makes this possible.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:05.700
So there is a script editor
associated with every sheet.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.190
And when you open up
the script editor,

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.519
you see the code
which runs it all.

00:09:11.519 --> 00:09:13.310
And there is a bunch
of functions in here--

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.390
add menu items, send
new offer letters.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:18.130
And I'll walk through
each one in turn.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.830
So add menu items
is the first one.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:24.690
And this is how the
workflows menu item got

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.970
added into your spreadsheet.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.814
Every Google Doc-- or I'm
sorry, every app script

00:09:29.814 --> 00:09:31.230
has this Current
Project Triggers.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.750
You can edit triggers
with your Apps Script.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.350
And here I'm saying when I
want to run the Add Menu items

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.220
function, when there's an event
from a spreadsheet on open--

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.390
so whenever anyone
opens the spreadsheet,

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:45.480
it will run this arbitrary
function that I made

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.340
and run this code.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:50.580
And what this code
is saying is give me

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.380
the UI, this spreadsheet
app UI, and create a menu

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.770
in the UI, the Workflows menu.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:59.190
And in that Workflows
menu, I want

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:02.610
to add an item whose text
is send new offer letters.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.830
And when someone clicks
send new offer letters,

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:07.440
it will run this function,
send new offer letters.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.180
And then I want to
add it to the UI.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.940
So send new offer letters--
what does that do?

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.390
In send new offer letters, we
get the active spreadsheet.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:18.090
Because this sheet is run from--

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.520
the script is run from a
spreadsheet when they clicked

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.840
the button in the UI, there's
an associated spreadsheet,

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.160
and you can get it, and then
pop up in that spreadsheet

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.731
a toast saying
sending offer letters.

00:10:28.731 --> 00:10:31.230
Then we get the active sheet,
which is the employee tracking

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.020
sheet, and the last row,
which is the last row of data

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:35.250
in the spreadsheet.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.700
We get the range of all of
the data that we care about--

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.590
the start row from the
start row and column

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.200
to the number of rows, which
is the last row minus 1,

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.877
and the number of columns
with the data we care about.

00:10:45.877 --> 00:10:47.460
Then we get the
display values of each

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.001
of these things,
which is how they're

00:10:49.001 --> 00:10:52.180
presented in the UI itself.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:55.630
Then we iterate through
each of the bits of data

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:57.910
and get the data for each row--

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:04.210
the row number, the name, the
email, salary, manager, team,

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:06.730
whether or not the
offer was sent.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:08.710
And if the offer
sent is not yes,

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:11.140
which is how I was able to
click it again and only send

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.031
new offer letters--

00:11:12.031 --> 00:11:14.030
if they haven't said yes
that an offer was sent,

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:15.490
then we create an offer letter.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.560
And I'll go through that
function in a moment.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:19.060
So after we create
the offer letter,

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:22.480
it gives us the ID
of what document

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.920
was created for
the offer letter,

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.290
and we create this URL--

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.854
docs.google.com,
document offer ID,

00:11:27.854 --> 00:11:29.770
and then a message that
we're going to email--

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.210
dear so-and-so, congratulations.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:31.871
You're hired.

00:11:31.871 --> 00:11:33.370
Please view your
offer letter, which

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.310
includes the next steps
at your offer URL.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:37.780
Sincerely, HR.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.200
It's a very customized message.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.720
So then we use Mail App, which
is one of the other apps that's

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:44.910
built into Apps Scripts.

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.380
There's many that
exist, and Maurice is

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:48.850
going to go through a few more.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.790
But Mail App is one
that you can send mail.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:57.220
So you say mail app, send
email with the message.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.770
The email is the user's email
address, the subject of You're

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.930
Hired, the message,
and just a little token

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.510
saying we don't want
people to reply to this.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:10.280
And then we get the range in
the spreadsheet saying yes, we

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:14.360
did send an offer, and here
is the URL of your customized

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.350
offer letter.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.850
We increment the
amount of letters

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.520
we sent, flush all of the
changes out to the spreadsheet

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.660
so it puts it in
the spreadsheet,

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:23.780
and then pop up a
toast saying either you

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.040
sent this many letters or
you didn't send any at all,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:27.980
because there was no
new letters to send.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.810
So how did we create
the offer letter?

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.540
So we have a
customized Google Doc,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.730
which is the template
of the offer letter.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.200
And in this template,
we basically

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.870
say welcome to the
next demo company.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.490
Hello, Name.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.110
We're thrilled to
offer you a job.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.930
Your starting salary
will be salary.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:48.360
Just basically a spreadsheet--
or I'm sorry, a doc with tokens

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:52.050
that we're going to
replace with customization.

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:53.970
So in the script, we--

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:58.380
using our Drive app this time,
we get the file of our ID

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.980
with the ID of our template,
and then make a copy of it.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.500
On the copy, we then
change the sharing,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.050
and we say this copy
can be visible to anyone

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.794
who has the link, but all
they can do is view it.

00:13:09.794 --> 00:13:11.460
And then we change
the name of that file

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.080
to say offer letter
for so-and-so.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.020
Then we use Document App,
which is the hook into Google

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:19.850
Docs within Apps Script.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:22.370
And we get the doc
by Document App,

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.382
and get the body
of the document,

00:13:24.382 --> 00:13:26.090
and then just do a
bunch of replacements.

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:28.940
We say for your name,
put the person's name,

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.180
for their salary, put the
person's salary, manager, team.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:33.950
For the NDA, it's a
little more complex,

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.650
because we want to
do text and URL.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:39.770
So we get the starting
point of where

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:42.050
we want to replace
text and generate a URL

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:45.980
and then say on this
portion within the document,

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.292
set the URL to
this NDA URL using

00:13:49.292 --> 00:13:51.500
this other function that
I'll go through in a moment,

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.420
generate NDA URL.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:55.580
And then return
the offer file's ID

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:59.240
so that the email that we
generated can link to it.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.610
So how do we
generate the NDA URL?

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.190
Because if you remember,
that was served,

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.000
I mentioned, by
Apps Script hosting

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.720
as a web app in this scenario.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:12.870
So generate NDA URL uses
this script app, yet

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.000
another thing that's
associated with Apps Script,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.790
which is the script
itself as a service.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:22.680
And you can get the URL of
the script app as a web app

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.560
and return this,
saying this is the URL,

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.390
and then append some parameters
to it saying here's your name,

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.690
here's your email address,
and here's your row.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.360
And you notice I use this encode
URI component, which is just

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:36.540
part of JavaScript, because this
is a JavaScript environment.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:41.080
So you can just call JavaScript
functions and make them work.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.224
And there's various other
bits in generate NDA URL,

00:14:43.224 --> 00:14:45.640
such as whether or not they're
actually signing it or just

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:48.340
viewing the NDA.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:52.610
So how do you get Apps
Script to act as a web app?

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:53.890
It's pretty simple.

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:56.740
You publish it by clicking
this Publish button, deploy

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:58.060
as web app.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:00.790
And then here you see do you
want to deploy it as a web app.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:03.250
And it gives you the
URL and gives you

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:06.340
some information of the version,
who it's going to execute as,

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.560
who can access the app.

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:10.710
And in order to
serve it, you just

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.960
create a function called Do Get.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:14.400
And Apps Script
will automatically

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.110
run this function
when someone hits

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.980
the HTTP request at that URL.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:23.100
And we can get parameters from
it, such as the URL parameters.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.730
We get the name, the email
parameter, the row parameter,

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.970
and the signed parameter.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:31.200
And we just do some
basic validation here,

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.380
saying whether or not--

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:35.820
if the request didn't have any
of these fields, we say sorry,

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:37.450
we couldn't verify
your employment.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:39.330
But if they did have
those fields, then

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.090
we want to hook into the
spreadsheet a bit more.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.650
So if you remember back
at the top of the example,

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:46.650
we used spreadsheet app,
get active spreadsheet,

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:50.520
because the spreadsheet was
associated with the script

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:53.130
since you were running the
script from the spreadsheet.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.260
In this scenario, you're
not running the script

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:56.730
from the spreadsheet,
so you can't

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.220
say get active spreadsheet.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.180
But you can just load
an arbitrary spreadsheet

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.980
with that spreadsheet ID.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.020
And then we say,
give me the sheet

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.360
by name of the employee
tracking sheet.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:10.260
And we get the row
that the employee said

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.060
that they were at from the URL.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.330
So in this scenario here,
we're not using great security,

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.547
because we just put an arbitrary
name, an arbitrary row.

00:16:18.547 --> 00:16:20.880
And someone could theoretically
change their row number,

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.213
and just troll through, and
try to figure out who you're

00:16:23.213 --> 00:16:24.840
hiring, what row they're on.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:26.700
In a better example,
you could imagine

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.680
that you could generate
a random number,

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.442
put that random number in your
spreadsheet, put it in the URL,

00:16:31.442 --> 00:16:33.150
and all of those things
have to match up.

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:36.510
But for demo purposes, this is
a little more straightforward

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.750
and easier to walk through.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.960
So here we get the values
of the data in that range

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:44.050
and verify that the name
they gave us and the URL

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:46.050
matches the name in the
spreadsheet at that row.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:49.350
And if it doesn't, we say sorry,
we couldn't verify things.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.150
If they've already
signed the NDA,

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:52.983
then we'd just say
you've already signed it.

00:16:52.983 --> 00:16:53.880
No more to do.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:55.830
But if they haven't signed it--

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.342
and this is the request
to do the signing--

00:16:59.342 --> 00:17:01.800
you said click here to sign
it, and they clicked it to sign

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:02.369
it--

00:17:02.369 --> 00:17:03.660
then we update the spreadsheet.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.329
We get the row, the column
that is the signing,

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:07.897
and say yes, you signed it.

00:17:07.897 --> 00:17:09.480
And you can see back
in the employment

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.750
that yes, they're signed,
and some other people have

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:12.250
signed it.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.530
And we spit back some
HTML saying thanks

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:19.510
for signing the NDA.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:22.270
If this is not the signing--
if this is serving of the NDA--

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.270
then we generate a URL
for actually signing it

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:29.230
and create some HTML output
saying here's an NDA.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.585
Click here to verify it.

00:17:30.585 --> 00:17:31.720
And that's it.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.910
It's less than 200 lines.

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:37.240
It's about 167 lines
and a full workflow

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:40.090
of how you can automate
with forms, sheets, docs,

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:43.050
create templates,
customize things, serve

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:46.780
Apps Script as a web app, and
do all of these things that

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:49.905
make life a lot simpler and
easier using Apps Script.

00:17:49.905 --> 00:17:52.030
And if you can go back to
the presentation, please.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:56.000
And that's it for me.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:56.584
I'm going to--

00:17:56.584 --> 00:17:59.124
Maurice is going to continue
for the rest of it for his demo.

00:17:59.124 --> 00:17:59.800
Thank you.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:00.924
MAURICE CODIK: Thanks, Sam.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:08.600
Another common process we
hear a lot about is reporting.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:10.940
So this is a sales
rep named Jane.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:13.940
And the end of the
quarter is approaching,

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:17.510
and it's time for her to prep
her quarterly business review.

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.390
So she needs to load up the
data on all her accounts.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:21.920
She wants to get it into Sheets.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:25.190
She needs to do some analysis,
and then finally, package it

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.080
together into a nice
presentation she

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:30.080
can give to her team
and to her leadership.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.420
Now, without any
kind of automation,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.130
this would be really tedious.

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:36.342
She'd have to dig up the data.

00:18:36.342 --> 00:18:37.800
She'd have to find
the presentation

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:38.700
she used last time.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.040
She would have to copy paste
the data from her new data

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:43.820
onto the old slides.

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:45.290
And it's really tedious.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:46.500
You can make mistakes.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.590
You can copy paste
the wrong numbers.

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:51.170
And at the end of the day,
it can look really bad

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:52.160
if you make a mistake.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:54.770
These mistakes are
not easy to catch.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:57.240
So using App Script and
the G Suite REST APIs,

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:59.550
we can make this
process a lot easier.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:01.670
We have a Google
Sheets REST API that

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.220
was designed to make it
really easy to import data

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.920
from external data sources
or third-party systems

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:08.750
into Google Sheets.

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.330
Last fall, we also
launched our first-ever API

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:14.630
for Google Slides so you
can take the data in Sheets

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:18.860
or another third-party system
and make a nice-looking slide

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:21.870
presentation with
some simple code.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:24.680
So let's walk through how we
can use these REST APIs together

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:26.180
to make Jane's job a lot easier.

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:32.220
So the first step is to get the
raw data into Google Sheets.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:35.330
Now, once it's there, Jane can
use the great analysis features

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:39.260
of Sheets, such as Explore
or all the functions that

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:42.000
come built in, to
understand the data,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:43.940
figure out what trends
she wants to highlight,

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.550
and visualize it in
a good-looking chart,

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:48.260
for example.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.000
So how do we get the data there?

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.980
Several large enterprise
partners have already

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:53.840
integrated with the Sheets API.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.440
And if you use some
of these tools,

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:59.300
you can take advantage of that
and get the data into Sheets

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:00.725
very easily.

00:20:00.725 --> 00:20:02.600
So earlier in the
conference, for example, we

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.300
announced that Salesforce
launched a new Sheets

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:08.810
integration where you can
import your opportunities

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:11.720
from Salesforce straight into
Sheets and update into Sheets,

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:14.810
and it will sync back and forth
between Salesforce and Sheets.

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:16.370
So if you're a
Salesforce user, you

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:19.726
can use that and get your
data into Sheets very easily.

00:20:19.726 --> 00:20:21.100
So let's say that's
not the case.

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:24.350
Let's say you have some
data in internal service,

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.260
an on-premises
database, something

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:30.050
where you are on your own.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:32.480
Thankfully, these APIs are
pretty easy to work with.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:35.600
You can write a quick
program using the Sheets API,

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:38.600
and take that data from
your on-prem database,

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:41.570
and put it directly into sheets.

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.770
So let's take a look
at how this code works.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:47.240
Can we switch to
the demo, please?

00:20:51.085 --> 00:20:51.710
So here we are.

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:54.200
This is a Python script I wrote.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.480
I won't go through
all of it in detail.

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:00.350
The bulk of it I copied from the
Google Sheets API Python Quick

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:01.880
Start.

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:04.100
And it has all of
this boilerplate

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:05.420
code in the beginning.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:08.840
That takes care of initializing
the client libraries,

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:13.370
going through authentication,
and making sure

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:16.070
that the user gives
consent for this app

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:19.040
to edit their spreadsheets.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:21.710
So this is boilerplate you can
get from our developer website.

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:26.190
I won't talk about
the bulk of that.

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:29.712
So here is the
core of our script.

00:21:29.712 --> 00:21:31.170
So the first thing
we do is we have

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.660
to get the data that we
want to put into Sheets.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:36.480
And here I have a
simple SQLite database

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.370
that's running on my laptop.

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:41.160
I have two tables,
customers, and orders,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.500
and I'm using some SQL
to join in together

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.750
and get the data
out that I need.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:48.586
So SQLite works
great for a demo,

00:21:48.586 --> 00:21:49.710
but this could be anything.

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:52.340
This could be a
big data warehouse.

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:54.760
This could be an
on-prem Oracle database.

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:58.680
This could be an
internal ordering service

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:00.990
that you need to call over HTTP.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:02.280
This could really be anything.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:08.170
Just plug it in with whatever
you need to get the data from.

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:10.690
So now let's go to the
sheet-specific parts

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:13.770
of the script.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:16.850
So in this mean method, I
call this load data function

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:20.030
that gets the data
from the database.

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.830
I add a header
row to the data we

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:26.300
get so that it's easier for a
human to work with afterwards.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:29.220
And then I start
calling the Sheets API.

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.760
So the first thing I do is I
call the spreadsheets create

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:36.290
method, which creates an empty
spreadsheet in Google Sheets.

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:40.070
Here I'm giving it the title
Imported From DB On Date

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:43.600
so that it's easy to pick
out from your drive later.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.000
And then after that,
I take the data

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.390
that we got from the database
and turn it into the request

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:52.300
that we eventually need
to send to Sheets to get

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.580
the data there.

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:58.150
So I do that with the
spreadsheets value Update

00:22:58.150 --> 00:22:59.970
Function.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.670
And for that, I give
it the spreadsheet ID

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.790
where in the spreadsheet I
want this data to end up,

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:09.510
so the range A1, just
the top left beginning

00:23:09.510 --> 00:23:11.220
of the spreadsheet.

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:12.780
I tell Sheets that
I want it to treat

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.340
this data as user-entered.

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.949
So it'll automatically--
it will behave as

00:23:16.949 --> 00:23:18.240
if you typed this data by hand.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:20.845
So it will understand that the
things that look like dates

00:23:20.845 --> 00:23:23.220
are dates, the things that
look like numbers are numbers.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:27.300
And again, we'll make this sheet
easier to work with at the end.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.559
I also tell Sheets that my
data is formatted in rows.

00:23:30.559 --> 00:23:32.475
Just because we read
this from a SQL database,

00:23:32.475 --> 00:23:33.690
it's naturally in rows.

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:35.280
It's easier to import.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.560
But if your data happens
to be shaped in columns,

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:41.500
you can say columns here, and
Sheets will do the right thing.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:43.470
So in a pretty quick
script, I think

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:47.040
I have 30 lines of
actual Sheets code.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:50.790
We can get data from
any data source,

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:55.500
practically, that the script
can talk to on Google Sheets.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:58.890
So let's give that a quick run.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.780
So it's just a Python script.

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:03.840
I hit Enter.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:04.890
And there we are.

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:09.320
So it put in the URL
of the spreadsheet.

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:10.430
I can load here.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:21.190
And I can open it up, and here's
the data from the database.

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:24.630
So I have the customers,
the name of each customer.

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.720
And each row is an order
that that customer placed--

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:29.670
so how many items
in their order,

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:31.110
the total dollar
amounts, and what

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.720
date the order was placed on.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.210
So based on this,
Jane can get started.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:40.920
She can do some analysis,
use some functions,

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.740
and get her work done.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:44.550
Back to the slides.

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:50.840
So the data is in
Sheets, and Jane can

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.950
get to work doing the analysis.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.520
So the next step is to make
this into a good-looking

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:58.140
presentation she
can give her team.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:01.070
So for this, we'll use
the Google Slides API,

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:02.600
and we'll call it
from App Script

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.550
so she can work with
the spreadsheet she's

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:08.700
working on and with the
slides at the same time.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:11.300
So using those things
together, in a few clicks,

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:13.850
we can get a good-looking
presentation.

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:17.080
So back to the demo, please.

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:17.580
All right.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:18.480
Great.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.862
So let's say Jane
did her analysis,

00:25:21.862 --> 00:25:23.070
and this is what she came up.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:28.200
She has a pivot table with the
top 15 customers she works with

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:31.950
and their sales per quarter
for the past year sorted

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:33.540
by their key force sales.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.570
She also has a chart that's a
little squished on the display,

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:43.530
but demonstrates how
the sales per customer

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:48.590
has changed throughout the year,
and some other configuration

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.680
data that we need to get the
slides ready-- so what year we

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.290
care about, what
quarter, her name,

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:56.540
things that aren't
necessarily in the data

00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:01.590
but end up in the
final presentation.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:03.650
So there are a couple
of ways you can trigger

00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:06.680
App Scripts from Google Sheets.

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:08.900
Earlier, Sam showed you that
we can add a custom menu

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:12.590
item where you could go
to the menu, click click,

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:15.090
and it will run and App
Script function for you.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:17.870
Here, I created a
button, because I think

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:21.180
big buttons are fun to push.

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:25.250
So if we click Generate Slides,
now this is running my script

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:27.560
and taking the data
from the spreadsheet,

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:30.600
combining it with a
template that we created.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:35.390
And in a few moments, we'll have
a nice, finished slide deck.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:39.837
So here's the link to the
presentation that we created.

00:26:39.837 --> 00:26:41.170
And this is what we end up with.

00:26:44.910 --> 00:26:49.300
So this is a simple quarterly
business review presentation.

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:52.800
It has the title slide with
the quarter we're reporting on,

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:53.430
the year.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.010
It has Jane's name.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:57.930
We have an Executive
Summary slide that

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.880
has the chart we came up with.

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:03.420
It has the total revenue
for the quarter, how much we

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:07.480
grew compared to our goal.

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:09.940
We have a table describing
the top five customers

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:12.490
and their quarter for sales.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:16.030
We have a slide with
the big, ambitious goal

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:18.500
to grow 12% next quarter.

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:20.230
And finally, we
have another slide

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:22.670
that has a link to
this spreadsheet

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:27.434
so that if you send this
presentation around,

00:27:27.434 --> 00:27:29.600
the people looking at it
can dive into the same data

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:32.200
that you worked on and
either learn more about it

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:33.510
or double check your work.

00:27:36.490 --> 00:27:43.280
So let's walk through the code
that powers that big button.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:47.300
So there's a lot that goes into
creating a slide presentation.

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:50.390
So for example, here we had to--

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:51.650
we chose a theme.

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:54.770
We had to make sure the
colors are on brand.

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:57.500
Sometimes, you want to
use some custom logos.

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:01.130
So instead of writing code
to define all these things,

00:28:01.130 --> 00:28:03.230
it tends to be a
lot easier to work

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:05.950
with a template presentation.

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:08.500
So this is the template that
we used for this example.

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:12.680
It has the five slides we'll
end up with at the end.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:16.000
And we're using some
variables in curly braces

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:17.920
that we'll use some
replacements that

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:22.870
get the data from the sheet
and into the presentation.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:26.610
So here we have a quarter
label, the year, the name.

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:30.570
On the executive summary,
we have the little variables

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:33.204
for the total sales,
the direction,

00:28:33.204 --> 00:28:34.620
and we have a
rectangle that we'll

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:39.190
use to replace the
chart into that spot.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.670
We have the table that the end
has the top five customers.

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:45.580
We have the slide
for a goal, and we

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:49.150
have a slide that we'll use
to link to the source data.

00:28:49.150 --> 00:28:54.230
So if you notice, the variables
already have styling on them.

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:58.570
So instead of having to specify
all the styles by hand in code,

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:01.150
you can make your slides
pretty close to what they'll

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:03.280
end up looking like at the end.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:05.230
You put the styling
on the variables,

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:07.600
and the Slides API
will keep that styling

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:11.720
when you do the replacements.

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:15.545
So let's look at the code
that creates the slides.

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:20.140
So the bulk of the work
happens in this Export

00:29:20.140 --> 00:29:21.139
to Slides function.

00:29:21.139 --> 00:29:22.680
This is the function
that I'm calling

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:26.680
when you click the big
button on the spreadsheet.

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:29.390
And the first thing we
do is tell the user, hey,

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:30.820
we're working on it.

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:33.760
We set some text on the ranges,
and we flush the changes

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:36.580
out to the spreadsheet
so that users know

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:37.705
that we're making progress.

00:29:40.215 --> 00:29:42.840
The next thing we do is we grab
the data out of the spreadsheet

00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:44.430
that we need.

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:46.320
Sam went through how this works.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:49.260
You basically use Spreadsheet
App to pick out ranges,

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:52.450
and you can get the displayable
values out of those ranges.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:54.450
So I'm not going to
cover that in detail

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.680
since we've already seen it.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:01.220
The next step is that
we use the Drive app.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:02.900
That's one of the
built-in libraries

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:04.130
inside of Apps Script.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.620
And that represents
your Google Drive.

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:10.880
So we have the ID
of this template.

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.790
This is the ID from the URL.

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:16.310
And we have it coded
into our script.

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.910
So we tell Drive app get me
this file, make me a copy,

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:21.390
please give it this title.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:24.140
So this-- we made a
copy of the template,

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.960
and then we'll use the Slides
API to modify that template

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:31.110
to produce the
final presentation.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:34.475
So how do we talk to
Slides from Apps Script?

00:30:34.475 --> 00:30:36.600
So here we're going to use
what's called the Slides

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:37.830
Advanced Service.

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.650
So an advance service
is a thin wrapped

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:46.207
around a Google REST API that's
available through Apps Script.

00:30:46.207 --> 00:30:48.540
There's a pretty large list
of advanced Google services.

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:51.420
You can go to Resources,
Google Advanced Services.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:56.300
And here's the big list of
the ones that are available.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:59.560
You see I have the Slides
API, and it's turned on.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.280
And a lot of these things are--

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:03.710
it's a lower-level API.

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:06.380
You get the full
power of the REST API.

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:08.880
So it ends up looking a little
different than the built-ins,

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:11.940
like Spreadsheet App, for
example, that was simpler.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:13.740
But here you get
a lot more power.

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:21.371
I should mention that Slides
Advanced service launched

00:31:21.371 --> 00:31:21.870
on Tuesday.

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:24.497
I actually turned it on while
I was on the flight over here

00:31:24.497 --> 00:31:25.080
from New York.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:31.920
So let's get to the
Slides-specific parts of this.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:35.200
So the Slides REST API works
using a batch update model.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:37.980
You basically assemble
a list of requests,

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:40.710
and you tell Slides please do
this, please do that, please

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:41.430
do that.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:44.130
You send the big batch
over the Slides API,

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.230
and all those
requests get executed

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:49.380
in order and in atomic fashion.

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:51.900
So the rest of the
script basically

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.030
goes through all
the things that we

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:56.190
want to do to the presentation.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:59.040
We assemble this big list
in Slides Requests, which

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:00.870
is just a JavaScript array.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:02.910
And then we call the
presentations batch update

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:06.980
method to actually
execute those changes.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:10.860
So I'll go through what the
steps are in a little detail.

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:14.180
So the first thing we do is that
we call over this Replace All

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:15.500
Text method.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:18.320
So earlier, we took the
data out of the spreadsheet,

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:21.360
and we added it to
the data object.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:23.720
And it's just a key
value of the things

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:27.020
we want to replace
in the template

00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:30.280
with the data we want
to replace it with.

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:33.400
So the request for that
looks pretty simple.

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:35.620
We look at all the
keys in the data map.

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:40.090
We create a little object that's
the Replace All Text request.

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:45.490
We say Slides, please take all
of the text that matches text,

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:48.400
and replace it with
this replace text.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.320
So this will go through
all of your slides

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:52.882
and put the data in
for all those variables

00:32:52.882 --> 00:32:53.840
we had in the template.

00:32:56.289 --> 00:32:58.330
The next thing we do is
that we call this Replace

00:32:58.330 --> 00:33:00.010
Shape With Chart API.

00:33:00.010 --> 00:33:01.510
So if you remember,
in the template,

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:04.750
we had a big rectangle
on the second slide

00:33:04.750 --> 00:33:07.120
that had the text YTD Chart.

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:10.240
And we'll use that
as the location

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:12.010
of where the chart will end up.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:13.990
So instead of having
to mess with--

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:17.540
I want the slide to be here, and
you have to nudge it a little--

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:18.196
sorry.

00:33:18.196 --> 00:33:19.570
Instead of messing
with the chart

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:21.236
and saying I want
this chart to be here,

00:33:21.236 --> 00:33:23.530
and fidgeting with
where to position it,

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:25.670
you can put a shape
in your template,

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.740
and then you can position
it where you want.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:29.190
You can see it in context.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.540
And you use Replace Shape
With Chart to actually put

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.580
the chart in that place.

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:36.800
So the request for
that is pretty simple.

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:39.530
You say I want to
replace the shape that

00:33:39.530 --> 00:33:43.400
contains this text, which is
just a YTD chart in our case.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:45.740
We tell it the spreadsheet
to grab the chart from,

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:47.770
which is just the
active spreadsheet

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:49.550
this script is being run from.

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:51.890
We give it the ID of the
chart, and we tell it

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:54.840
we want this chart to be linked.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:57.320
So what I mean by
linked is that slides

00:33:57.320 --> 00:34:01.170
will remember that this chart
came from this spreadsheet

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:05.750
so that if the underlying
data in Spreadsheets changes,

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:08.449
there will be an update button
in Slides that you can click,

00:34:08.449 --> 00:34:12.500
and it will refresh the
chart automatically.

00:34:12.500 --> 00:34:14.900
So that way, your data
is always up to date.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:18.050
There's also an API
on the Slides API

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:21.131
to call refresh on
a chart for you.

00:34:21.131 --> 00:34:23.630
So if you're working with both
the sheets API and the Slides

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:26.210
API, you can change
some data in Sheets

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:28.430
and then tell slides,
hey, there's a new chart.

00:34:28.430 --> 00:34:29.179
Please pick it up.

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:37.409
So the final thing we do
is that we add a link.

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:40.340
From the last slide, we add
a link to the source data

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:43.889
so people can take a look
at the underlying data that

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:47.090
went into the presentation.

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:49.650
So the first APIs
that we worked with,

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:52.630
they worked in a find
and replace manner.

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:55.750
So we just gave them
the text in a shape,

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:58.690
and the API found it
and did the right thing.

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:00.100
Some of our other APIs--

00:35:00.100 --> 00:35:01.780
are Object ID based.

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:05.020
So I have to give slides
the ID of the object that

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.920
contains the text that
we want to add a link to.

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:12.780
So to do that, we call
the presentations Get API

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:17.960
right here, and we fetch the
presentation from slides.

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:20.090
We pick out the final
slide, and then we

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:22.980
iterate through all the
elements on that slide.

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:24.170
So we know that slide.

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:24.920
We created it.

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:28.120
It's a title and body slide,
so it has two shapes on it--

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:30.950
a box for the title
and a box for the body.

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:34.420
And I iterate through
the two shapes on there,

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.777
and I find the one that
is the body placeholder.

00:35:36.777 --> 00:35:39.110
So we know that's the one
that has the text that we want

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:44.150
to linkify, so we pass the
object ID of that shape

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:47.990
into this update link function.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:51.710
So Update Link is
a function that

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:57.020
updates the textile of that
box to add a link style to it.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:02.060
So we do links and
slides or textiles.

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.830
If you wanted to do
other textile changes,

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:05.538
like you want to make
the font different,

00:36:05.538 --> 00:36:07.190
you want to make
it bold or italics,

00:36:07.190 --> 00:36:08.773
you would do it in
a much similar way,

00:36:08.773 --> 00:36:09.650
using Update Textile.

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:15.160
So that's all the
requests that we

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:19.390
needed to make the slides
look the way we want.

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:22.810
So we replaced all our
variables, we added the chart,

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:26.780
and then we added the
link to the source data.

00:36:26.780 --> 00:36:29.150
So finally, we
call batch update,

00:36:29.150 --> 00:36:32.000
and then we tell the user
we generated your slides.

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:33.620
Here's when we did it.

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:39.870
And we add the URL to the
presentation back to sheet.

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:43.890
So in all, it's probably
174 lines of code,

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:46.560
including the Sheets
stuff I didn't cover.

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:50.040
But with that, we created
a good-looking presentation

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:54.230
using the data in the
spreadsheet using Apps Script.

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:55.487
Back to the slides, please.

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:02.190
So using these APIs
in G Suite, Jane

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:04.260
saved several hours of her time.

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:07.050
She avoided having to do
all this manual copy paste,

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:10.050
and now she's ready to give
this presentation to leadership.

00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:12.030
So that saved time.

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:15.060
She can get to work meeting
her next quarter goals,

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:19.260
or she can go outside and enjoy
the sunshine a little bit.

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:22.394
So we broke up the steps
here to demonstrate

00:37:22.394 --> 00:37:23.310
what your options are.

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:25.900
But really, you could
automate this a lot further.

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:28.960
So for example, you
could have the import

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.320
to sheet script run
automatically every quarter.

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:32.250
You can have it--

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:34.230
you can say-- once
the data is there,

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:36.870
you can send an email to Jane
saying hey, your data is ready.

00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:38.910
Please go take a
look and get started.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.530
You could-- if
you wanted to, you

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:43.980
could skip Sheets altogether
and have your program that

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:46.260
queries the database do
the analysis for you,

00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:48.840
and then go straight to
Slides, because Slides is also

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:51.400
REST API that you can call
from a third-party tool.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:58.310
So in summary, with
some creativity

00:37:58.310 --> 00:38:00.110
and just looking
around, I think you

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:02.570
can find a lot of workflows
in your enterprise

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:05.630
that can be automated with App
Script and the G Suite APIs,

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:08.390
similar to the ones
we've seen today.

00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:09.800
And these are small automations.

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:11.966
These are things that you
can write in an afternoon.

00:38:11.966 --> 00:38:14.180
You can write it in
a couple of days.

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:17.800
But even though they're
small, you can--

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:19.310
the savings add up.

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:21.710
If you do the same
thing for every hiring

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.430
manager, for every
party organizer,

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:25.610
for every sales rep
in your company,

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:28.070
the dollar savings just
multiply and overall can

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:31.380
make a big impact.

00:38:31.380 --> 00:38:35.300
So the demos we did show you,
they do require some coding.

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:36.920
It's pretty simple.

00:38:36.920 --> 00:38:40.524
Most of it is just JavaScript.

00:38:40.524 --> 00:38:42.440
We showed some Python
code today that was also

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:44.330
pretty simple to write,
and those libraries

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:47.750
are available in pretty
much any major language.

00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:49.460
So it does require some coding.

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:50.600
It's simple.

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:53.270
It's within the reach of power
users and IT professionals.

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:55.340
But it's not for everybody yet.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:57.860
So if you're looking
for a low code way

00:38:57.860 --> 00:38:59.720
to do these kind of
automations, there's

00:38:59.720 --> 00:39:01.460
a G Suite product
called App Maker that's

00:39:01.460 --> 00:39:03.460
currently in early access.

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.870
And that provides
a drag-and-drop UI

00:39:05.870 --> 00:39:10.520
that lets you create apps
without really doing much code.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:13.960
So if you're interested in that,
you can request access to App

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:17.740
Maker at
developers.google.com/appmaker.

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:19.616
And they also have a
showcase base at Mosconi

00:39:19.616 --> 00:39:21.031
through the end
of the conference.

00:39:21.031 --> 00:39:23.020
If you find them, you
can learn a lot more

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:25.870
about how that works.

00:39:25.870 --> 00:39:27.910
So another good
resource for learning

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:31.540
about these automations is
the transformation gallery.

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:34.060
It's a gallery that lists
several real world use

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:36.790
cases of the G Suite APIs--

00:39:36.790 --> 00:39:40.960
and not even just the APIs, but
the applications themselves--

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:42.400
and how you can
use them together

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:44.650
to transform your business.

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:47.939
So a lot of them come
with customer stories

00:39:47.939 --> 00:39:49.480
so you can see what
the real business

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:54.430
impact was of deploying these
integrations in real life.

00:39:54.430 --> 00:39:56.641
You can check it out
at transformationga

00:39:56.641 --> 00:39:59.530
llery.withgoogle.com.

00:39:59.530 --> 00:40:01.240
So wrapping up--

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:04.210
App Script is a lightweight
tool that lets power users

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:07.630
and IT professionals
write simple JavaScript

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:10.790
to automate actions on top
of many Google services.

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:14.380
So today, we showed you how to
script with Docs, with Slides,

00:40:14.380 --> 00:40:17.480
with Sheets, with
Gmail, with Drive.

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.095
But App Scripts supports
many other Google services

00:40:20.095 --> 00:40:21.220
that we didn't cover today.

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:24.500
So for example, there's
a library for Calendar,

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:27.040
for Translate, for
YouTube, for Maps.

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:29.080
And there's also that
list of advanced services

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:32.080
that give you more
powerful interfaces that

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:36.670
are easy to use to
call Google REST APIs.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:38.760
So in general,
it's easy to learn.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.020
It runs in the cloud,
and it's available to you

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:43.060
as part of your G
Suite subscription.

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:44.650
It is part of the
glue that helps

00:40:44.650 --> 00:40:47.680
make G Suite into a true suite
of apps that work together.

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:52.880
So there are also REST
APIs for Sheets and Slides

00:40:52.880 --> 00:40:56.060
that make integration with
third-party data sources easy.

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:59.890
So they provide simple ways
to get your data into G Suite,

00:40:59.890 --> 00:41:01.580
where you can
collaborate on it, you

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:05.190
can script it using Apps Script,
you can present it to others.

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:07.460
So using App Script
and our Rest APIs,

00:41:07.460 --> 00:41:09.290
you can automate
many of the tedious

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:12.050
process in your business,
save time and money,

00:41:12.050 --> 00:41:17.970
and let folks focus on creating
value instead of busywork.

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:20.334
So to learn more about the
things we covered today,

00:41:20.334 --> 00:41:22.000
you can check out our
developer websites

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:24.240
for Apps Script,
Sheets, and Slides,

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:26.850
and the URLs are on
the screen right now.

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:28.740
There's a lot of
documentation available,

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:32.040
and there are plenty of samples
to help you get started.

